s to India gaining independence in 1947.\n\nYet, despite the global condemnation and subsequent investigation into the Amritsar Massacre, the British government has never issued a formal apology. Decades later, while visiting the Golden temple in Amritsar, then Prime Minister David Cameron acknowledges the atrocities as 'monstrous,' but falls short of giving an official apology.\n\nAs we approach the centenary of this tragic event, the debate over whether Britain should finally apologize for the Amritsar Massacre continues. Supporters argue that offering an apology would be a significant gesture towards healing historical wounds, while skeptics question the purpose such an apology would serve today. Regardless of where one stands on this issue, the Amritsar Massacre remains a poignant reminder of the human capacity for cruelty and the importance of never forgetting our history."}]}
