 this article:"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Okay little buddy, let me explain this to you. Sometimes, there are people who are supposed to take care of and protect others, but instead, they do very bad things. In a place called Rotherham, a group of people did bad things to many young girls. Some people who work for the government might have known about it and tried to hide it from everyone else. They did this by getting rid of important papers that had information about the bad things happening. The people in charge are going to look into this very carefully to find out the truth and make sure that everyone is safe."}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant, who always provide explanation. Think like you are answering to a five year old."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Police Detain Student Gunman After Moscow School Shooting\n\nUpdated Feb. 3, 2014 12:38 p.m. ET\n\nMOSCOW--Frantic parents came to collect their children, police swarmed and walled off the premises and crowds huddled outside a pink-and-white school in the freezing cold, shivering in the aftermath of a violent shooting by a student.\n\nIt's a scene all too familiar in the U.S. after tragedies such as Columbine and Sandy Hook, but until Monday such a harrowing sequence of events largely hadn't transpired in modern Russia, a country already on high security alert days before hosting its first Winter Olympic Games.\n\nFifteen-year-old straight-A student Sergei Gordeyev stormed into Moscow's School 263 mid-day in a long women's fur coat that concealed two rifles, and proceeded to shoot his geography teacher point-blank in the chest and head before taking a classroom hostage, fellow student Lyudmila Arkhipova recalled in a Russian television interview.\n\n\"He said he wanted to know if there is life after death,\" Ms. Arkhipova, who was among the hostages, told Russia's LifeNews. \"It was clear that he was really scared and was not himself.\" She said he was also carrying a bag with extra bullets and a knife.\n\nSoon, Mr. Gordeyev's mother and grandmother began calling on his mobile phone to try to calm him down, and eventually his father showed up at the classroom and talked his son out of the situation, Ms. Arkhipova said. \"(His father) literally saved our lives,\" she said. In a separate interview, she said his father talked him out of killing himself.\n\nBut for some inside the school it was already too late: Geography teacher Andrei Kirillov, 29, died on the spot, and Mr. Gordeyev shot also two policemen when they arrived at the scene, killing one and wounding another, Investigative Committee Spokesman Vladimir Markin said in a statement.\n\nMr. Gordeyev has been detained and is being investigated for hostage-taking, murder and assault on law-enforcement officers, Mr. Markin added.\n\n\"Investigators are questioning the detainee with the help of experts,\" he said. \"They are investigating the reasons that impelled the teenager to commit the crime. Psychological and psychiatric examinations have been ordered, and investigators will soon decide the question of restrictive measures against him.\"\n\nHigh-profile school shootings are nearly unprecedented in Russia. Monday, Moscow Mayor Sergei Sobyanin ordered an immediate check of safety measures at the capital's schools. The incident comes nearly a decade after a group of terrorists took a school hostage in the southern city of Beslan, leaving hundreds dead.\n\nSome officials on Monday began to blame the incident on a corrupting American influence.\n\n\"I can barely remember something like this ever happening,\" Valery Ryazansky, a senator in Russia's Federation Council, told the Interfax news agency. \"We're not America, where a school shooting happens practically every day. This teenager probably got an eyeful of these American plots.\"\n\nAt a meeting with cultural officials on Monday in the Russian city of Pskov, President Vladimir Putin urged the country to better teach its young generation to understand and value theater, drama and music. \"Maybe if we did that in the right way, the kind of tragedies like the one in Moscow today wouldn't happen,\" he said. Mr. Putin did not elaborate on the shooting.\n\nFor students at the Moscow school, the event brought unprecedented shock. Fifteen-year-old Anton Alifatov was in the middle of Russian class on the third floor when the events began unfolding two floors below. He said the students suddenly heard a pop.\n\n\"We thought something probably had fallen,\" Mr. Alifatov recalled. Then another teacher came into his classroom to break the news, and everyone began to evacuate into neighboring school and local government buildings, he said.\n\nOutside the school--a three-story building sandwiched between Soviet-era block apartments in a working-class part of northeast Moscow--a picture began to emerge of a tall-and-skinny top achiever who had excelled in Russia's high-pressure physics and math culture and showed few if any signs of violent behavior.\n\n\"He's very smart, outgoing enough,\" said 16-year-old classmate Anna Dmitrieva, who didn't go to school Monday but came to the building with her twin sister once she heard the news. \"He didn't have a ton of friends because he studied a lot.\"\n\nMs. Dmitrieva described him as a tall teenager with curly brown hair who looked skinny but actually was known among his classmates for being able to do a lot of push-ups and pull-ups. She said she believed he intended to go to a top university and become a physicist.\n\nA number of students, including Ms. Dmitrieva, said he didn't seem to have any sort of known conflict with the geography teacher who was killed.\n\nShe said Mr. Kirillov, was \"great.\" \"I would say he was one of the best teachers in the school,\" Ms. Dmitrieva said.\n\nRussia's Ministry of Education released a statement saying Mr. Kirillov leaves behind a wife and a young baby. \"Students loved him, colleagues greatly respected him. Thanks to his heroism, this teacher was able to save tens of lives of children and adults,\" the Ministry of Education said.\n\nThere are signs that Mr. Gordeyev was familiar with guns. \"He told me about how he went to a shooting range and learned how to shoot, and said that he did very well and his father brought him there,\" Roman Klimenko, a classmate who was also among those taken hostage, said in a television interview with LifeNews.\n\n\"He told his dad that he wanted to go shooting, to learn,\" Mr. Klimenko said. \"It was a normal training firing range. Where he got the weapon from (on Monday) isn't clear.\"\n\nMr. Gordeyev's family could not be reached for comment.\n\n—Olga Razumovskaya contributed to this article.\n\nWrite to Paul Sonne at Paul.Sonne@wsj.com\nWhat are the most important parts of this text?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "A 15-year-old student named Sergei Gordeyev brought two rifles to his school in Moscow and shot his geography teacher. He then took a classroom hostage. Eventually, his father went to the school and talked him out of the situation, helping everyone in the classroom. Unfortunately, the teacher passed away and two policemen were injured. People were very scared and shocked by this event because school shootings are very rare in Russia."}]}
