{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are an AI assistant. You will be given a task. You must generate a detailed and long answer."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Answer the following question:\n\nprotein that serves as the precursor molecular for thyroid hormones?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The precursor molecule for thyroid hormones is Thyroglobulin, a large glycoprotein that serves as the scaffold for the synthesis of thyroid hormones, primarily thyroxine (T4) and a smaller amount of triiodothyronine (T3). These hormones are essential for regulating various physiological processes like metabolism, growth, and development throughout the body.\n\nThyroglobulin is synthesized within the thyroid gland by thyroid follicular cells, also known as thyrocytes. After being synthesized, it is stored in large quantities within the colloid of the thyroid follicles. Thyroglobulin is comprised of 660 amino acids with a molecular mass of approximately 330 kDa. It contains several tyrosine residues, which play a crucial role in the synthesis of thyroid hormones.\n\nThe biosynthesis of thyroid hormones involves a series of steps:\n\n1. Iodide uptake: Diet-derived iodide ions are actively transported from the bloodstream into the thyroid follicular cells through a membrane-bound transporter called the Sodium/Iodide Symporter (NIS).\n\n2. Iodide oxidation and organification: Once inside the follicular cells, the iodide ions are oxidized to reactive iodine by an enzyme called thyroid peroxidase (TPO). TPO catalyzes the binding of this reactive iodine to the tyrosine residues in the thyroglobulin molecule through a process called organification or iodination.\n\n3. Coupling: The iodinated tyrosines within the thyroglobulin molecule are then coupled together to form either T3 (containing three iodine atoms) or T4 (containing four iodine atoms). Once again, this reaction is catalyzed by thyroid peroxidase.\n\n4. Endocytosis and proteolysis: When thyroid hormones are