es to one's diet. By incorporating foods that are believed to help lower blood pressure, such as skim milk, spinach, beans, bananas, and dark chocolate, Jill can improve her blood pressure levels and reduce her risk of developing cardiovascular complications.\n\nIn addition to dietary changes, it would be beneficial for Jill to engage in regular exercise and, if needed, take prescribed medication as advised by her healthcare professional. On the other hand, Beth, who has normal blood pressure, might not need to make any significant dietary changes. However, maintaining a balanced diet, exercising regularly, and monitoring her blood pressure would be good preventive measures for her to ensure continued cardiovascular health."}]}
