idn’t jump on the car because they wanted to. They got hit, and it was a natural reaction [to get onto the hood].” \n  \n Despite the momentary mayhem, Ebrahem said, “I just want people to know that the rally was peaceful.” \n  \n Soon after leaving the intersection, Rice pulled over and called 911, police spokesman John Elder said Wednesday. No one else was hurt, according to police. \n  \n City officials said Wednesday that organizers did not need a permit to hold their protest, despite concerns about the potential disruptions to traffic. \n  \n Elder said police were still “actively investigating” the incident before turning the case over to the Hennepin County attorney’s office to decide whether charges are appropriate. |||||\n\nSummary:"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Two Minneapolis rallies protesting the grand jury's decision in the Ferguson case drew several hundred people on Tuesday, including a scary incident where a car struck a protester and then proceeded to burst through a pack of surrounding protesters. The woman who was hit suffered minor injuries and was taken to the hospital. The driver called the police shortly after the incident to report it, and no one was arrested. The rallies were held to show solidarity with Michael Brown, the black teenager shot and killed by a white police officer in Ferguson.\n\nDemonstrators sat down for 4 ½ minutes of silence at the University of Minnesota's Coffman Union to symbolize the 4 ½ hours that Brown's body reportedly lay in the street after he was shot. The protesters carried signs saying \"Black lives matter\" and \"No justice, no peace\" while calling for an end to racial discrimination. Additional rallies were planned in St. Paul and Duluth in a continued call for justice and an end to systematic injustice.\n\nThe driver, Jeffrey Patrick Rice, who hit the 16-year-old girl during the protest, claimed that he was \"attempting to flee from the mob\" when the incident occurred. Rice was not arrested, and no