ion parameter (μ), the scale parameter (σ), the skewness parameter (β), and the index of stability (α). Stable distributions provide a generalization of several well-known distributions, including the Gaussian distribution, Cauchy distribution, and Lévy distribution.\n\nThe mean of a stable distribution can be very different from the mean of a Gaussian distribution. Stable distributions are defined by their characteristic functions. Importantly, stable distributions have finite values for mean and variance when the index of stability α > 1. When 0 < α < 1, the mean and variance can be infinite and thus not well-defined. When α = 2, the stable distribution converges to a Gaussian distribution, so the mean would be the same. For 1 < α ≤ 2, the mean of a stable distribution will generally differ from the mean of a Gaussian distribution, as the properties of the distribution depend on the location, scale, skewness, and the index of stability parameters.\n\nTo answer your final question, no, questions 1 and 2 are not asking the same thing. Question 1 is asking about the difference between Gaussian and normal distribution, which are essentially two names for the same distribution. Question 2, on the other hand, is exploring the comparison of the mean values between stable distributions and Gaussian distribution under a specific condition (when the index of stability α >1). These questions are about distinct aspects of the probability distribution concepts in statistics."}]}
