great potential, to be admitted to prestigious colleges, is through an admissions officer who is willing to risk their career.\n\nHowever, the author of the article points out that admissions officers in reality are much smarter than the film suggests and are always looking for promising students who may have been overlooked by others. The film's satire serves as a reminder that although institutions like Princeton offer excellent education, obsessing over gaining admission to such elite colleges often leads to disappointment. The movie emphasizes that acceptance into a prestigious college does not guarantee a happy and successful post-college life.\n\nThe film also addresses the issue of college rankings and the pressure on institutions to maintain their positions, with the director of Princeton admissions complaining about a downgrade in their U.S. News & World Report ranking. The film urges for a sense of perspective in the competitive world of college admissions, arguing that the value of an education cannot be solely measured through rankings and prestige."}]}
