w contains high concentrations of waste products such as urea, creatinine, and excess electrolytes. This concentrated fluid, now referred to as urine, moves into the collecting ducts and then into the renal pelvis, the central funnel of the kidney.\n\n5. Ureters carry urine out of the kidneys: From the renal pelvis, urine flows into the ureters, which are muscular tubes that transport urine to the urinary bladder. Through the process of peristalsis, rhythmic muscular contractions, the urine is actively pushed through the ureters towards the bladder for temporary storage.\n\nIn conclusion, the missing first step in the process presented is: blood entering the kidneys through the renal artery. This step is vital for the initiation of filtration and waste removal in the renal system."}]}
