loyed in the study of social psychology, where it may be examined in conjunction with subjects such as deviance and sanctioning. \n\nIn summary, opprobrium refers to a deep form of public disgrace or criticism, often resulting from an individual's or group's actions that are seen as highly reproachable, immoral, or unethical. The experience of opprobrium can have severe personal and societal consequences, affecting one's reputation and relationships within their community."}]}
