izen through marriage, you must be willing to revoke any citizenship you currently have. Contact the embassy or consulate of your country of origin to find out what you need to do to officially revoke your citizenship. To apply for citizenship, your husband must complete a form and supply documents to the GDRFA that prove your identity and indicate your desire to become a citizen of the UAE. You will have to pay a fee for the request to be processed. You must be present with your husband when he submits the form and documents."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "In the United Arab Emirates (UAE), women do not enjoy equal citizenship rights as men. Female Emirati citizens who marry non-Emirati men do not automatically pass on their citizenship to their husbands or children, making them ineligible for the benefits that Emirati citizens enjoy. Women's citizenship is considered dependent on their husbands or fathers, regardless of their employment status or independent living situation. As a consequence, male spouses of Emirati women are unable to apply for UAE citizenship through marriage, irrespective of the duration of their union.\n\nOn the other hand, non-Emirati women who marry Emirati men can apply for UAE citizenship after being married for at least three years. Spouses of Emirati citizens, regardless of gender, are allowed to reside in UAE, but do not have the rights and benefits of citizenship. In order to apply for UAE citizenship through marriage, individuals must be willing to revoke their current citizenship as the country does not recognize dual citizenship.\n\nWhen seeking to apply for UAE citizenship via marriage, couples must visit the General Directorate of Residence and Foreign Affairs (GDRFA) with their marriage certificate and identification documents. They must complete the required forms and pay a processing fee. Husbands of non-Emirati women must submit additional documents to prove their spouse's identity and intent to become a UAE citizen.\n\